      "Language": "chi",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Endogenous descending inhibitory system plays an important role in the modulation of nociceptive transmission. In recent years, descending facilitatory system has been noted. CNS bi-directionally modulates the peripheral nociceptive transmission at the spinal cord level via the descending inhibitory/facilitatory system. Serotonin (5-HT) is the main neurotransmitter in descending pain modulation. Intrathecal application of 5-HT or electrical stimulation or glutamate microinjection into the nucleus raphe magnus (NRM) and nucleus reticularis gigantocellularis(NGC) may produce inhibition and/or facilitation of spinal nociceptive transmission. The contradictory results may be attributed to the nonselective action(s) of 5-HT at multiple subtypes of 5-HT receptor in spinal cord."},
